## How the Test Suite Is Designed

**114 tests, 8 test files, 0 network calls.**

Tests never call the LLM. A `conftest.py` fixture forcibly clears the `OPENAI_API_KEY` before any test runs, even if you have a real key set. All extraction uses static fixture data. Tests run in under a second.

The test data comes from factory functions in `tests/fixtures.py`. Each one builds a fake deed object with the sample data baked in. You can override any field with a keyword argument, so tests stay focused: if you're testing date logic, you only change the dates; everything else stays at defaults.

Here's what each test file covers, in plain English:

---

### `test_pipeline_integration.py` — The Big Picture Test

This is the acceptance test. It runs the **entire pipeline** on the exact OCR text from the assignment, end to end.

| Test | What it checks |
|---|---|
| **result is invalid** | The sample deed must be rejected, not accepted |
| **exactly three errors** | Not 2, not 4 — exactly the 3 known bugs |
| **DATE_ORDER_ERROR present** | The flipped dates (recorded before signed) are caught |
| **APN_FORMAT_ERROR present** | The letters in "992-001-XA" are caught |
| **AMOUNT_MISMATCH_ERROR present** | The $50K digit-vs-words gap is caught |
| **difference is 50K** | The error reports the exact discrepancy: $50,000 |
| **no UNKNOWN_COUNTY_ERROR** | "S. Clara" matched successfully — no false alarm |
| **no INVALID_STATUS_ERROR** | "PRELIMINARY" is a valid status — no false alarm |
| **no STATE_CODE_ERROR** | "CA" is a real state — no false alarm |
| **no NOT_A_DEED_ERROR** | The sample IS a deed — preflight must pass |
| **clean deed passes** | A fixed version of the deed (dates corrected, amounts matching, numeric APN) goes through the pipeline and isn't rejected |

If this file passes, the project does what the brief asked for.

