## Key Design Choices (What Reviewers Care About)

**Why not let the LLM validate?**
Because LLMs are probabilistic. If both dates are in January 2024 and look plausible, an LLM might say "looks fine." Python date comparison doesn't have opinions — `Jan 10 < Jan 15` is a fact.

**Why collect all errors instead of stopping at the first one?**
So the user can fix everything in one pass. Discovering problems one at a time wastes everyone's time.

**Why typed error classes instead of strings?**
Machine-readable errors let you build on top of this. Route `AmountMismatchError` to a different review queue than `DateOrderError`. Filter by field name. Serialize to JSON for an API. Strings can't do any of that.

**Why compute closing costs last?**
A deed with bad dates or a dollar mismatch should never get a cost estimate. Computing costs on corrupt data would be misleading. Closing costs only appear when all 16 checks pass.

**Why does the preflight step exist?**
If someone types "hi" or pastes a recipe, there's no reason to waste an API call on LLM extraction. A quick keyword scan catches obvious non-deed input in microseconds.

**Why SHA-256 hash the input?**
Traceability. The hash of the original OCR text is embedded in every extracted deed. If someone re-runs the pipeline on modified text, the hash changes. You can always trace a result back to its exact input.

